    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d397">

                <pb facs="0537" n="423" xml:id="pg423"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.01/139: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Kellogg</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Extract]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1924-11-18T17:00:00+00:00"><hi rendition="#italic">November 18,
                                1924—5 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received November 18—3:04
                        p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>479. Department’s 413, November 12, 6 p.m.<anchor n="46" xml:id="d397-f1"/>
                    Department’s November 12, 6 p.m., to Peking was read to Foreign Office today
                    which stated that the reply of the British Government to the French
                    representations made here was in substance identical with the reply of Secretary
                    Hughes. Foreign Office added you may sum up our position in the words, “We agree
                    with the diplomatic body in Peking in a wait and see attitude.”</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Kellogg</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
